<onlyinclude>'''Social information processing''' refers to how people notice, interpret and use information about other people and social situations. It also names distinct theories in developmental psychology and computer-mediated communication.

== Explanation ==
Someone who is accidentally bumped may interpret the event as hostile or harmless, depending on the cues available and previous experiences. Online, people may form impressions gradually from text and other communication cues. These are different research traditions with related terminology.

